System Sequence Diagrams
I chose “Schedule an Appointment” and “Request a Refill for a Prescription” to show sequence diagram examples for the Patient Tracker System.
Schedule an Appointment:
Actor: Patient
Object: AppointmentSystem, Calendar, ProviderNotification
Steps of this interaction:
1.	The patient requests a date and time for their appointment.
2.	The patient tracker system checks the provider’s availability in the Calendar.
3.	The calendar returns whether that appointment is available.
4.	The system approves the appointment and stores it.
5.	The system then notifies the provider of the appointment.
6.	The patient gets confirmation from the system when the provider accepts the appointment.
 

Request a Refill for a Prescription:
Actor: Patient
Object: RefillSystem, Provider, Pharmacy
Steps of this interaction:
1.	The patient requests a refill for their prescription.
2.	The RefillSystem will check whether the patient is due for a prescription refill. 
3.	If the patient is due for a refill, the RefillSystem will send the refill request to the Provider. If the prescription can’t be refilled, the RefillSystem will send an error message back to the patient and deny the request. 
4.	 The Provider will see the request and approve the refill.
5.	The RefillSystem will send the request for a refill to the Pharmacy.
6.	The Pharmacy will accept the request and refill the prescription.
7.	The RefillSystem will notify the patient that the refill request has been sent to the Pharmacy and can be picked up there. 
 
Activity Diagrams
Schedule an Appointment:
States:
-	Initial State: The patient logs onto the patient tracker system.
-	Final State: 
1.	 The appointment is accepted.
2.	The appointment is denied because it isn’t available.
Actions:
The patient pulls up the calendar to view the provider’s availability. The system brings up the provider’s calendar, and the patient can choose a day and time that works for them. 
 
Request a Refill for a Prescription:
States
-	Initial State: The patient logs onto the patient tracker system.
-	Final State: 
1.	The prescription is refilled.
2.	The prescription is unable to be refilled.
Actions:
The patient selects the prescription they would like refilled. The system then checks whether the prescription is eligible to be refilled or not. 
 





Project Planning and Development Approach:
Software 
•	Frontend: React.js • 	Backend: Python 
•	Database: MySQL 
Hardware 
•	Computers 
•	A server 
Network 
•	Quick internet access will be ideal, so patients can get the most up-to-date information about their health quickly. 
•	The network will be protected by firewalls/encryption, so that patient health information is not breached or shared with people who should not have access. 
Development Plan: 
•	Weeks 1–2: The database will be set up, and the backend will be connected to the frontend. 
•	Weeks 3–4: User registration will be implemented, and login for different users (patients, doctors, admins, etc.) will be tested. 
•	Weeks 5–6: Patient account management will be set up so they can update information as it changes. 
•	Weeks 7–8: The appointment scheduling and the reminder system will be created. 
•	Weeks 9–10: Administrator management capabilities will be added, allowing them to access patient and staff accounts. 
•	Weeks 11–12: The system will be tested, and any errors will be fixed. 
•	Weeks 13–14: Usability and user experience will be improved. 
•	Week 15: Final testing will be performed, and the system will be prepared for release. 
 

